A||||5||Linx||crimped a.c. hose||||1||
Z||000000||Linx||08-09-2000||12:00 PM||||Doggone it!  I'm pokin' around under my car and I've discovered a crushed a.c. hose.  (dadgum that prior owner!)  Can an a.c. hose be repaired? We're talkin' the rigid ones that run under the vehicle.  I'm betting i wont be able to find a new one.  Any suggestions?<P>   <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/linxramblr/crushed.jpg">    <P><BR>And oh...my A.C. doesnt work.  I just finished rebuilding the compressor and found this shortly thereafter.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-09-2000||12:26 PM||||Your sure that's AC? seems awfully large in the pic..... <P>The metal line can be fabricated. I'm not sure where you can get the raw stock for a car. I guess you could always use stainless for it if the correct aluminum isn't available.<P>I would not try to patch there with soft line. Technically it would probly hold but I'd worry about it getting hit and bursting. Or melting, looks kind of clost to the exaust.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Linx||08-09-2000||03:27 PM||||I guess I Should have mentioned that this crimp is at the front of the car. that bar in the pic is the sway bar. thats the fuel tank in the background.||||reg||1
Z||000003||HugGRRRFiero||08-09-2000||03:30 PM||||  If that view is looking rearward, the bar at the top is a crossmember under the car.  You've got a crushed A/C line.  Don't know of any fix or where you can find that A/C line, maybe someone does.<P>Brent<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">    <BR>    <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-09-2000||08:24 PM||||explains why it looked weird.<P>you could patch that with soft line there. You want to figure out a way to protect it a bit. Maybe by putting some stainless braid over it. That would protect it from small stuff.<P>I don't know where in your area you can get that done.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||08-10-2000||09:59 AM||||Take it to a welding shop, have 'em cut or grind just the DENT out, and then patch the pipe. In other words, don't actually seperate the pipe, just cut a hole in it to remove the dent. Then they can weld an aluminum plate over the hole left by the cutout section. Should be a simple quick procedure, and probably less costly than the alternatives. If you want, they can probaly even use a thicker piece of metal that might be more resistant to a repeat of that kind of damage.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
